{
  "dom_id": "step-q74038",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ and $u\\cdot v=-2,$ then $\\frac{u\\cdot (10v)}{u\\cdot (4u)}=$",
  "worked_solution": "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $u\\cdot (10v),$ we multiply $u\\cdot v$ by $10.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (10v) & =10(u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $u\\cdot (4u),$ we compute $u\\cdot u$ first and then multiply the result by $4.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (4u) & =4(u\\cdot u) \\\\  & =4({u}_{1}^{2}+{u}_{2}^{2}+{u}_{3}^{2}+{u}_{4}^{2}) \\\\  & =4((-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-1)^{2}) \\\\  & =4(1+4+1+4+1) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 11 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{u\\cdot (10v)}{u\\cdot (4u)}=\\frac{-20}{44}=-\\frac{5}{11}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
